{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c383","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ae75-a6d1-7e71-949a-c0aa2731b1b5"},"sequence":28,"recorded_at":1780531400000051,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"37dc71c1-9569-4ea8-89d7-d279257fe1a3","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"37dc71c1-9569-4ea8-89d7-d279257fe1a3","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"37dc71c1-9569-4ea8-89d7-d279257fe1a3"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ae75-bf24-78d2-b2cf-071cb9d0a7ec"},"task_id":"01a0ae75-bf24-78d2-b2cf-071cb9d0a7ec","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ae75-bf24-78d2-b2cf-071cb9d0a7ec","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-1-1\",\n  \"command\": \"task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2atgj4, status=development)' 2>&1 | head -n 50; echo \\\"---CP1---\\\"; cat /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-2atgj4/TASK-260830-2atgj4_producer.md; echo \\\"---CP2---\\\"; cat /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-2atgj4/TASK-260830-2atgj4_republish-rev1.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Set status and read task instructions\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"{\\\"ok\\\":true,\\\"result\\\":{\\\"primary\\\":{\\\"action\\\":\\\"status_changed\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"TASK-260830-2atgj4\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"to-dev\\\"},\\\"side_effects\\\":[{\\\"action\\\":\\\"escalated\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"STORY-260830-1oqfec\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"integrating\\\"},{\\\"action\\\":\\\"escalated\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"EPIC-260830-1uwj54\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"to-review\\\"}]}}\\n---CP1---\\nOwn TASK-260830-2atgj4 (property-test-ownership-reducer), the FINAL leaf of STORY-260830-1oqfec (ownership-leases-and-fencing). Predecessors on the Story branch: 2f5393 (internal/sessrepo lease_store.go: CreateLease, CompareAndSwapLease, WinningLease, VerifyFencingToken, CheckFencingExpiry, CompareLeaseTuple agreeing with sessstate.Compare — now an exhaustive 144-pair agreement test) and 3g12yp (internal/fencing: Authorize* gates, sealed LeaseToken, terminate-stale, park vocabulary; ownership registry re-pin) are accepted and checkpointed. Prove, as executable property tests over the LANDED production reducers (internal/sessstate Input.Union / Compare / the projection that selects the reported winner and records conflicts; sessrepo.WinningLease/ListLeases; fencing.Authorize*), the four pinned v0.6.0 invariants named by the task under Sections 2.2, 5.3 and 13.6-13.10: (1) union-order independence — for any multiset of lease/event inputs (including duplicates and off-chain competing leases) every permutation and every partition into successive unions yields byte-identical projected state (winner, conflicts, divergent history), (2) stale and same-epoch loser preservation — a losing lease (lower epoch, or same epoch losing the tuple order) is never dropped, rewritten or promoted; its history remains visible as divergent/conflict evidence after any sequence of unions, force takeovers and reconnects, (3) clock non-authority — created_at and any wall-clock member never influence the winner, the fencing decision or the parked/refused outcome (shift every timestamp arbitrarily, including backwards and equal, and assert identical verdicts), (4) zero duplicate authorized owners — after any admissible sequence, at most one lease is authoritative per logical session and per epoch, and the fencing gates admit exactly that one; two hosts or two processes can never both pass an activation-class gate for the same session. Use Go's testing/quick or rapid-style generators you write yourself (no new module dependency unless the go-testing-tools skill lists one; then justify) over a closed small alphabet (epochs 1..4, holders a/b/c, lease ids x/y/z, hosts h1/h2, event kinds the landed shapes admit) with exhaustive enumeration where the space is small (state it) and bounded random exploration with a fixed seed and a recorded iteration count otherwise; every property must be falsified by at least one narrowing mutant of the production reducer (winner tie-break inverted; loser dropped from the union; created_at consulted; a second authoritative owner admitted at the same epoch) executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control — a property that no plant can falsify is not evidence. Also add the P3 vector from the 3g12yp verdict (TestAuthorizeRefusesMalformedPresented: {session A, epoch 0, valid third lease} expecting invalid_arguments) and reword the stale section:17.2 gap prose to its narrower reader-enum meaning if the registry is touched. No product behavior change is expected; if a property exposes a defect, fix it minimally with a RED-first regression and say so. This is the FINAL leaf (story_final CR): carry the Story-close items — traceability registry acceptance cases for the four properties bound to the real reducers with honest coverage text, reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256 re-pinned; README sections for internal/fencing (if missing) and the property suite with test commands and no capability claim; LOGBOOK entry. Managed Story worktree contract: .temp/STORY-260830-1oqfec/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1oqfec whose tip is the 3g12yp checkpoint; leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED, never commit on the Story branch, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept; if origin/main moves before your handoff (it will: a release-agnostic validation command is landing on trunk), run task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-2atgj4 and restore any trunk-changed path the refresh left stale with a single-path git checkout HEAD